<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#nomadic-confederation">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nomadic confederation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/209-bce">209 BCE</a> – <a href="/years/1757">1757 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The nomadic confederation was the steppe’s answer to the farming empires on its edges: herding tribes joined under one ruling clan and khan, organised as a single army and sustained by plunder, tribute and trade.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Together with his administrative division of Transoxiana into districts and his currency reform, this was the most serious attempt to turn the khanate from a <b>nomadic confederation</b> into a territorial state with consistent administrative capacity, but his successors lacked the will or the means to continue it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han military combined a conscript infantry base with a professional cavalry arm developed specifically to counter the Xiongnu <b>nomadic confederation</b>, the empire's most persistent strategic threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xianbei emerged from the ruin of the Donghu, the 'Eastern Hu', a <b>nomadic confederation</b> of the eastern steppe that Modu, chanyu of the Xiongnu, destroyed around 206 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>For a <b>nomadic confederation</b>, the Xiongnu state was an elaborate political organisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
